{
  "id": "fluid_flow___equation_of_continuity",
  "title": "Fluid Flow - Equation of Continuity",
  "description": "Calculator for the Equation of Continuity based on conservation of mass in steady flow processes. Calculates output velocity for fluids with uniform density through pipes and ducts using the formula: vout = vin × Ain / Aout",
  "type": "datatable",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "id": "continuity_calculator",
      "content": "Equation of Continuity Calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "vin",
          "label": "Input Velocity (vin)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m/s",
          "defaultValue": 0.35
        },
        {
          "id": "ain",
          "label": "Input Area (Ain)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m²",
          "defaultValue": 0.00785
        },
        {
          "id": "aout",
          "label": "Output Area (Aout)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m²",
          "defaultValue": 0.00503
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "vout",
          "label": "Output Velocity (vout)",
          "unit": "m/s",
          "formula": "inputs.vin * inputs.ain / inputs.aout",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "qin",
          "label": "Input Flow Rate (q)",
          "unit": "m³/s",
          "formula": "inputs.vin * inputs.ain",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "qout",
          "label": "Output Flow Rate (q)",
          "unit": "m³/s",
          "formula": "inputs.vout * inputs.aout",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "datatable",
      "id": "continuity_examples",
      "content": "Example Calculations",
      "headers": [
        "Scenario",
        "Input Velocity (m/s)",
        "Input Area (m²)",
        "Output Area (m²)",
        "Output Velocity (m/s)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "100mm to 80mm pipe",
          "0.35",
          "0.00785",
          "0.00503",
          "0.55"
        ],
        [
          "Pipe expansion",
          "2.0",
          "0.005",
          "0.010",
          "1.0"
        ],
        [
          "Pipe reduction",
          "1.0",
          "0.010",
          "0.005",
          "2.0"
        ],
        [
          "Large diameter change",
          "0.5",
          "0.020",
          "0.005",
          "2.0"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "continuity_explanation",
      "content": "## Equation of Continuity\n\nThe Equation of Continuity states that for a steady flow process with uniform density:\n\n**vin × Ain = vout × Aout**\n\nOr rearranged:\n\n**vout = vin × Ain / Aout**\n\nWhere:\n- **vin** = input velocity (m/s)\n- **Ain** = input area (m²)\n- **vout** = output velocity (m/s)\n- **Aout** = output area (m²)\n\nThis principle applies to incompressible fluids like water, oil, and other liquids flowing through pipes, tubes, and ducts. When a fluid flows from a larger diameter pipe to a smaller one, the velocity increases to maintain constant flow rate."
    }
  ]
}